Add sensor widths for cameras missing from the upstream OpenMVG database.

NOTE ON KEY FORMAT -- read before adding entries here.

openMVG_main_SfMInit_ImageListing looks up EXIF "Make" + " " + "Model" (see
main_SfMInit_ImageListing.cpp:343) and Datasheet::operator== matches in two steps:

  1. The first space-delimited token of the database key must equal the first
     token of the query, i.e. the key MUST start with the EXIF Make.
     A bare "Chameleon3 CM3-U3-50S5M" key has maker token "chameleon3" and can
     never match a query of "Point Grey Research Chameleon3 CM3-U3-50S5M".
  2. The FIRST digit-containing token of the key must appear verbatim as a token
     of the query. So the key must not carry a digit-bearing family name ahead of
     the model code: "Point Grey Research Chameleon3 CM3-U3-50S5M" reduces to the
     token "chameleon3", which every Chameleon3 shares, and a CM3-U3-31S4C entry
     would then silently claim a CM3-U3-50S5M camera. Use
     "Point Grey Research CM3-U3-50S5M" so the discriminating token is the model code.

Lookup takes the FIRST match in file order, so appending can add a match but can
never override an earlier entry. "Pixel 3a" is shadowed by upstream's
"Google Pixel 3a;5.76" (line 2007) and cannot be corrected by appending.

The bare keys below are kept because pgs_recon's Python importers
(reconstruct.py:79-82, pgs_data.py:173-176) do an exact dict lookup of
"{make} {model}" and then fall back to "{model}" alone. They are inert for the
C++ matcher. Do not add '#' comment lines to the database itself -- sfm_utils'
openmvg_load_camdb() regex rejects them and logs a parse error.

